  "query_preview": "..Temperatures will remain a few degrees above\nnormal the next couple of days thanks to the ridge centered just\nto the east. An Extreme Heat Warning is still in effect until 8\nPM this evening. PWAT values are elevated running around 150 \npercent of normal with expected CAPE this afternoon 500 \nJ/kg-1000 J/kg. Expect another round of scattered showers and \nthunderstorms from Tucson south and eastward. HREF members show \nslightly more coverage compared to yesterday with Tucson on the \nwestern edge of activity. Plenty of DCAPE will also be in place \nwith some mid-level drying as well as mid level winds begin to \nturn westerly in advance of a Pacific low. Because of this \nstorms will be capable of wind gusts 40-50 mph with a few storms\npossibly producing severe wind gusts. The 25/00Z HREF shows a \n10 percent chance for wind gusts in excess of 50 kts within 40 \nkm of a point over much of Graham and Greenlee Counties late \nafternoon and evening. Outflows may also generate blowing dust \nover dust prone locations east of Tucson. Storms will also be \ncapable of locally heavy rainfall and hail in the stronger \nstorms. Friday moisture decreases further with a slight chance \nfor storms along the AZ/NM border. \n\nA strong low for this time of year moves into the Pacific\nNorthwest tomorrow with the core of the low moving through the\nGreat Basin this weekend into early next week. The troughing\nwill extend down into Arizona bringing temperatures down to\naround normal Sunday into next week. The main impact the low\nwill have is to increase winds starting Friday into early next\nweek. Expect speeds 15-25 mph with gusts 30-40 mph. The\nstrongest winds will likely occur Saturday and Sunday. The\nstrong winds, along with low RH and high fire danger will create\nareas of critical fire weather conditions from central Pima\nCounty eastward each afternoon both days. Because of this the\nFire Weather Watch has been upgraded to a Red Flag Warning.\nFriday and Monday fire weather conditions will be elevated. \n\nNext week the upper air patter stays amplified over CONUS with \nridging over the eastern half and troughing over the west coast.\nThis will keep temperatures around normal through next week.\nMid-level flow may become southerly by mid-week, although some\nmodels show more of a southwesterly flow over Arizona which\nwould keep moisture to the east. Depending on how this plays \nout Arizona could see another push of moisture leading to the \npotential for increased storm activity.",
